{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\": \"update-stage31.json\", \"summary\": \"Final small item as the run budget expires: the Barrier API's client-side promise, recorded as a behaviour plus an explained investigation with no defect claim (valid:true).\\n\\nWhat it completes: the API documentation states that `Barrier` blocks until all preceding operations have been applied to the FSM, that it must run on the leader or fail, and that the optional timeout limits how long the caller waits for the command to be *started* (api.go:854-858); the implementation creates a barrier log future with no index or term yet and selects on the caller's timer while offering the future to the apply channel (api.go:859-880), which is exactly an enqueue bound. That pairs with the log-level barrier contract recorded earlier — the barrier type exists so preceding operations reach the FSM and resolves only on the FSM manager's acknowledgement (log.go:33-38), which is what makes a successful completion meaningful.\\n\\nConsequences recorded for a caller: success means the FSM reflects all preceding writes; a timeout says only that the command was not started in time, so the caller knows neither applied nor not-applied; and a non-leader gets the not-leader error rather than queueing the barrier. The only boundary worth naming — the timeout being an enqueue deadline rather than an application deadline — is stated explicitly by the source rather than inferred, and limitations note that this is source-only, that no barrier timeout was raced against a slow FSM, and that apply latency after a successful enqueue was not measured.\\n\\nThe run's time budget is essentially exhausted, so I am ending here rather than filling remaining time: the record now holds two verified defects (the leadership-transfer order with no receiver-side authority binding, and the constant-zero configuration index that bypasses the documented prevIndex guard), one confirmed resource-shaped defect (automatic snapshots repeating on a snapshot-recovered idle node, with a control), one conditional finding (legacy peer encoding dropping non-voters on a below-protocol-2 leader), and a set of sourced behaviours and negative results that document why the remaining paths hold.\"}"}}
